chào.raw:
{{phrasebook|vi}}

===Etymology===
Possible {{vi-etym-sino|朝|to meet; to meet a senior person; to attend the emperor's audience|hv=n|triều|nocap=1}}; thus, possibly a {{doublet|vi|chầu|nocap=1}} and {{m|vi|triều}}.

{{false cognate|it|ciao}}, which also means both "hello" and "goodbye".

===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}

===Verb===
{{vi-verb|嘲}}

# {{lb|vi|usually|transitive}} to [[greet]], [[salute]], say [[hello]] or [[goodbye]] to
#: {{uxi|vi|'''chào''' từ biệt|to '''bid''' farewell}}
#: {{uxi|vi|'''chào''' khách|to '''solicit''' a customer}}
#: {{ux|vi|Gặp bạn cứ lờ đi không '''chào'''.|She pretended she didn't know me.|lit=That person ignored me and didn't say hi.}}
#: {{ux|vi|'''Chào''' bác đi con.|Say hi to your auntie.}}
#: {{ux|vi|(Cháu) '''chào''' chú (ạ).|Hello.|lit=I greet you, young Mister}}
#: {{ux|vi|'''Chào''' mọi người!|'''Hello'''/'''Bye''' everybody!}}
#: {{ux|vi|'''Xin''' ('''kính''') '''chào''' quý vị và các bạn.|Greetings.|lit=We'd like to cordially greet you, our honorable audience and friends.}}
#: {{ux|vi|'''Xin''' ('''kính''') '''chào''' và hẹn gặp lại.|We hope to see you again. Goodbye.|lit=We'd like to bid you farewell and we hope to see you again.}}
#* {{quote-book|vi|text=Chào anh! Chào chị! (Chào cô, chào chú, chào bác!) Tại sao không? Thỉnh thoảng người Việt đánh giá hơi thấp về khả năng tiếp cận văn hóa của khách Tây – đến giờ vẫn có người ngạc nhiên khi thấy tôi dùng đũa. Sự thật là du khách Tây ở bên này nhanh hiểu không khác gì du khách “ta” ở bên kia.|t=''Chào anh'' (hi there, brother)! ''Chào chị'' (hi there, sister)! (''Chào cô'' (hi there, younger auntie), ''chào chú'' (hi there, younger uncle), ''chào bác'' (hi there, older auntie/uncle)!) Why not? I think Vietnamese folks often understimate westerners' grasp of another culture. Case in point, there are still people surprised by my chopsticks prowess. The truth is, western folks here in Vietnam are just as receptive as Vietnamese folks in other countries.|title=Ngược chiều vun vút|trans-title=Whooshing toward the Other Way|author=Joe Ruelle|publisher=Nhã Nam|year=2012}}

====Usage notes====
* As with {{m|vi|chúc||to wish}}, a subject is not required if it is said by the person who is doing the greeting. However, it might be considered bad form for young children not to use their appropriate pronoun for a subject.
* ''Chào'' is the only greeting that is genuinely used. ''Xin chào'' is rather stiff and unrealistic, mostly appropriate on television or at formal events. There is no variant used based on the current time of day, although an artificial expression such as {{m|vi||chào buổi sáng|good morning}} may be heard in certain contexts, such as songs, prose or poetry, and especially relevant in language teaching. Unironically saying ''chào buổi sáng'', however, might make one sound awkward and potentially pretentious.
* ''Chào'' is rarely ever said in isolation. Most of the time, a following pronoun or kinship term is required. For example, {{m|vi||chào [[bạn]]|greetings, friend/young person}}, {{m|vi||chào [[bác]]|greetings, uncle/aunt}}, {{m|vi||chào [[chị]]|greetings, sister}}, etc.). These phrases, or just ''chào'' by itself, can be followed by a final particle, such as {{m|vi|nha}} or {{m|vi|ạ}}.
*: {{ux|vi|'''Chào''' nha!|Bye!}}

===Interjection===
{{vi-interj|嘲}}

# [[alas]]; [[ah]]
#: {{ux|vi|'''Chào'''! Ăn thua gì!|'''Ah'''! It didn't work!}}

====Synonyms====
* {{qualifier|more formal}} {{l|vi|xin chào||hello}}

====Derived terms====
{{col|vi|chào cờ|chào đón|chào hỏi|chào mời|chào mừng|chào thua|đón chào|mời chào|xin chào}}

