# {{lb|vi|chiefly|_|Central Vietnam|_|and|_|Southern Vietnam}} [[I]]/me {{q|used when talking to someone significantly older than the speaker}}
# [[you]] {{q|used by parents when talking to their children}}
# {{lb|vi|chiefly|_|Central Vietnam|_|and|_|Southern Vietnam}} [[you]] {{q|used when talking to some significantly younger than the speaker}}
#: {{ux|vi|Là '''con''' thật!|It's '''you''' for real!}}

====Usage notes====
* Sense (4) is chiefly used in Central and Southern Vietnam, perhaps extensively to North Central Vietnam. In Northern Vietnam, {{m|vi|cháu}} is used instead. Some Northerners, however, do use ''con'', especially when talking to Southern children on Southern TV shows.

====Synonyms====
* {{s|you (4)}} {{vi-l|cháu}}

===Classifier===
{{vi-cls|[[𡥵]], [[昆]]}}

# {{ng|Indicates animals (including humans).}}
# {{lb|vi|disrespectful}} {{ng|Indicates female people.}}
#: {{ant|vi|thằng}}
#: {{uxi|vi|một thằng, hai '''con'''|one guy, two girls}}
# {{ng|Indicates knives, ships, boats, trains and [[pupil#Noun_2|eye pupils]].}}
#: {{uxi|vi|'''con''' dao|a knife}}
# {{ng|Indicates roads, rivers, streams and waves.}}
#: {{uxi|vi|trên '''con''' đường đến hạnh phúc|on the road/path to happiness}}
# {{lb|vi|somewhat|literary}} {{ng|Indicates written characters.}}
#: {{uxi|vi|'''con''' chữ|a character or letter}}
# {{lb|vi|colloquial}} {{ng|Indicates any piece of technology, especially with the brand name.}}
## {{ng|Indicates electronic devices.}}
##: {{uxi|vi|'''Con''' Iphone của em đấy.|This is my Iphone.}}
## {{ng|Indicates wheeled vehicles.}}
##: {{ux|vi|Anh mày có hẳn hai '''con''' xe Honda đấy nhớ!|I have two Honda motorbikes!}}
## {{ng|Indicates video games and movies.}}
##: {{ux|vi|Ông chơi '''con''' game này chưa?|Have you played this game?}}

====Usage notes====
* Even though ''con người'' is used, it is generally thought of as a noun phrase on its own, and {{m|vi|người}} does not require a classifier because it is itself a classifier (compare {{cog|ja|人|tr=nin}}). ''Một '''con''' người'' "a person" does not sound dehumanizing, but even literary, while ''một người'' sounds casual enough.
* The phrase ''con người'' is popularly employed as a philosophical trope or device to bring up discussions about what it means to be human as opposed to being an animal, even though it is not really semantically convincing given the fact that humans are, zoologically, animals, and there are non-animal things going with this classifier.

