nhất.raw:
===Alternative forms===
* {{l|vi|nhứt}}
* {{l|vi|nhít}}

===Etymology===
{{vi-etym-sino|一|{{l|en|one}}}}.

The ‹nh-› is from {{IPAfont|ʔj-}}, which was perceived as the implosive {{IPAfont|*ʄ-}} then developed into {{IPAfont|ɲ-}} as implosives nasalized: {{IPAfont|ʔjit > *ʄit > ɲit}}. For a similar case of development of Middle Chinese {{IPAfont|ʔj-}}, compare {{m|vi|nhân}}, from {{ltc-l|因}}. See Ferlus (2009) for details.

Sino-Vietnamese shows fairly systemic contrast between characters belonging to Chongniu III and IV rime groups with certain initials: ''nhất'' (< {{IPAfont|*ʔj-}} < {{ltc-l|一}}) vs. {{m|vi|ất}} (< {{IPAfont|*ʔ-}} < {{ltc-l|乙}}), {{m|vi|dân}} (< {{IPAfont|*j-}} < {{IPAfont|*mj-}} < {{ltc-l|民}}) vs. {{m|vi|mân}} (< {{IPAfont|*m-}} < {{ltc-l|珉}}), {{m|vi|tần}} (< {{IPAfont|*bj-}} < {{ltc-l|嬪}}) vs. {{m|vi|bần}} (< {{IPAfont|*b-}} < {{ltc-l|貧}}). The first character of each pair belongs to Chongniu IV group and shows clear palatalization in its Sino-Vietnamese reflexes, while the second belongs to Chongniu III and yields simplex initial.

