===Alternative forms===
* {{vi-l|bánh mỳ}}

===Etymology===
From {{com|vi|bánh|t1=pastry, cake, bread, dumpling, noodle, wafer, or pudding|mì|t2=wheat}}.

A [[folk etymology]] claims that {{m|vi|bánh mì}} is a corruption of {{ncog|fr|pain de mie}}; however, this is obviously false, see the entry for {{m|vi|bánh}}. An 1898 dictionary defines {{ncog|fr|miche}} as {{m|vi|bánh tây}}, a now-dated term for Western-style bread.<ref>{{cite-book|fr|title=Dictionnaire franco-tonkinois illustré|trans-title=Illustrated Franco-Tonkinese Dictionary|author=Pierre Gabriel Vallot|location=Hanoi|publisher=Schneider|year=1898|page=228|url=https://hdl.handle.net/2027/hvd.hwubz7?urlappend=%3Bseq=264}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}
* {{audio|vi|LL-Q9199 (vie)-Jessica Nguyen (Pamputt)-bánh mì.wav|a=Ho Chi Minh City}}

===Noun===
{{vi-noun|[[𥹘𫗗]], [[餅麵]]|cls=[[chiếc]], [[ổ]], [[cái]]}}

# [[bread]], especially [[Saigon]]-style short [[baguette]]s with filling
#: {{uxi|vi|'''bánh mì''' ngọt|sweet bread}}
#: {{uxi|vi|bánh mì Pháp|baguettes}}
#: {{uxi|vi|'''bánh mì''' (kẹp thịt)|"sandwich baguette"; baguette with filling}}
#: {{ux|vi|'''Bánh mì''' Sài Gòn, một ngàn một ổ!|Saigonese banh mi, one thousand dong a loaf!}}
#: {{uxi|vi|bò kho bánh mì|beef stew with bread}}
#: {{ux|vi|Ăn '''bánh mì thịt''' hoài chán quá. Bữa nay ăn '''bánh mì ngọt'''.|I'm tired of pork-filled baguettes. Imma have sweet bread today for a change.}}
#* {{see citations|vi}}

====Usage notes====
* The classifier {{m|vi|chiếc}} is used in northern Vietnam, while {{m|vi|ổ}} is used in southern Vietnam.<ref>{{cite-web |title=Archived copy |url=http://www.lib.washington.edu/SouthEastAsia/vsg/elist_2009/Banh%20mi.html |accessdate=19 April 2014 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20151028235951/http://www.lib.washington.edu/SouthEastAsia/vsg/elist_2009/Banh%20mi.html |archivedate=28 October 2015 }}</ref>
* As a popular breakfast food, it often means Saigonese short baguettes with pork or beef filling, although vendors may still have to clarify with signage with "''bánh mì thịt''" that they sell this food specifically rather than sweet and soft forms of bread. Bread shops, especially those of swanky foreign bread franchises, may have more sweet and soft bread items than Saigonese baguettes.

