===Etymology===
From {{inh|vi|mkh-vie-pro|*ʔəːt}}.

{{w|Pigneau de Béhaine}}'s ''Dictionarium anamitico-latinum'' (1772 manuscript) glosses ''ớt'' as ''{{lang|la|pimentum}}'',<ref>{{cite-book|author= w:Pigneau de Béhaine|year= 1772|title= Dictionarium anamitico-latinum|page= 367 of 662|pageurl= https://archive.org/details/DictionariumAnamiticoLatinumPigneaux/Dictionarium%20Anamitico-Latinum%20-%201772%2C%20P.J.%20Pigneaux/page/n395/mode/2up}}</ref> a {{w|Neo-Latin}} derivation from French {{m|fr|piment||‘spice’ → ‘chilli pepper’}}.

{{lw|vi|Phạm Đình Hổ}}'s 1827 dictionary ''{{lw|vi|Nhật dụng thường đàm}}'' ({{lang|zh|日用常談}} "Common Words Used Daily") glosses ''ớt'' {{lang|vi|乙}} as {{lw|zh|蓽澄茄|蓽䔲茄}} (SV: ''tất đăng gia''; "[[tailed pepper]], [[cubeb]], {{w|Piper cubeba}}"),<ref>{{cite-book|author= w:vi:Phạm Đình Hổ|year= 1827|title= {{lw|vi|Nhật dụng thường đàm}} ({{lang|zh|日用常談}})|trans-title= Common Words Used Daily|chapter= ớt|chapterurl= https://web.archive.org/web/20230721133650/http://www.nomfoundation.org/common/show.php?detail=1202&uiLang=en}}</ref> also called {{m|vi|tiêu thất}} in Vietnamese; so that plant was probably ''ớt''’s original {{w|referent}} before the introduction of chilli pepper from the Americas.

===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}

===Noun===
