gió.raw:
===Etymology===
From {{inh|vi|mkh-vie-pro|*k-jɔːʔ|*k-jɔːʔ ~ *kʰjɔːʔ}}, from {{inh|vi|aav-pro|*kjaːl|*kjaːl ~ *kjɔʔ|wind, air}} (Shorto 2006, #1782). Cognate with {{cog|mtq|xỏ}}, {{cog|aem|-}} ''kajɑːʔ'', {{cog|pac|cudyial}}, {{cog|km|ខ្យល់}}, {{cog|bdq|kial}} and {{cog|sat|ᱦᱚᱭ}}.

Attested in ''Phật thuyết đại báo phụ mẫu ân trọng kinh'' (佛說大報父母恩重經; {{circa2|12th cent.|short=1}}) as {{m|vi|𪬪}}, composed of {{ltc-l|个}} + {{ltc-l|愈}} (modern [[Sino-Vietnamese|SV]]: {{m|vi|cá}} {{m|vi|dũ}}), and {{ltc-l|愈}} (modern [[Sino-Vietnamese|SV]]: {{m|vi|dũ}}). Attested in the ''Annan Jishi'' (安南即事, 13th century) as {{ltc-l|教|id=2}} (compare the Sino-Korean and Sino-Japanese readings of the character). Attested as {{m|mkh-mvi|gió}} in the ''Dictionarium Annamiticum Lusitanum et Latinum'' (1651).

The rime of the Vietic and Munda forms (with back, rounded vowel) does not really match with the rest of Austroasiatic (which has {{IPAfont|*-aːl}}) and is probably one of the few identifiable Vieto-Munda isoglosses. Compare {{cog|jun|-}} ''kojo'', {{cog|scb|-}} [Rục] ''kəjɔː³'' vs. {{cog|omx|kyāl}}, {{cog|tdf|-}} ''kjaːl''.

