kim.raw:
===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}
* {{audio|vi|LL-Q9199 (vie)-Penn Zero MSSJ-kim.wav|a=Hanoi}}

===Etymology 1===
{{vi-etym-sino|金|[[metal]]; [[gold]]}}.

This reading is irregular, the expected reading would be ''*câm''; cf. {{m|vi|cấm}} < {{ltc-l|禁|id=2}}, {{m|vi|cầm}} < {{ltc-l|琴}}. ⟨-im⟩ is a very rare rime in Sino-Vietnamese proper, with this and the homophonous ''kim'' < {{ltc-l|今}} being the only examples.

It is possible that the usage of ''kim'' as the Sino-Vietnamese reading for {{ltc-l|金}} and {{ltc-l|今}} was to avoid homophony with {{m|vi|câm||mute}}, although it is difficult to be sure. The much less common {{ltc-l|衿}} (that basically has no usage in Vietnamese) is considered to have the expected reading {{m|vi|câm}}.

Also possibly compare {{cog|ko|금}} and {{m|ko|김}}, the former is the expected Sino-Korean reading of {{ltc-l|金}} while the latter found chiefly as a surname.

The reading {{m|vi|câm}} is attested in the work ''Đắc thú lâm tuyền thành đạo ca'' ({{lang|vi|得趣林泉成道𰙔}}), attributed to {{w|Trần Nhân Tông}} ({{lang|vi|陳仁宗}}) and included in the book ''Thiền tông bản hạnh'' ({{lang|vi|禪宗本行}}; 1745). This reading is found in the [[gatha]] at the end of text and is rhymed with {{m|vi|心|tr={{vi-l|tâm}}}} and {{m|vi|隂|tr={{vi-l|âm}}}}. It is also attested in the book ''Tam tự kinh giải âm diễn ca'' ({{lang|vi|三字經解音演歌}}; 1888), where it is rhymed with {{m|vi|擒|tr={{vi-l|cầm}}}} and {{m|vi|音|tr={{vi-l|âm}}}}.
<!--- I suspect that this is a posthoc projection of what the "regular" pronunciation would be, i.e. it's the same thing as ''*câm'' above: a non-existent hypothetical form. Obvious the composer of the dictionary did not know of the convenient asterisk for hypothetical forms back then.
[[File:金 Câm.jpg|thumb|In Nam Hoa tự điển, 金 is listed as having the reading of câm, however it lists kim as its common reading.]] -->

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|vi|câm||rare}}

====Noun====
{{vi-noun}}

# [[metal]], one of the {{w|Wu Xing}}
#: {{ux|vi|Thổ sinh '''kim'''. '''Kim''' sinh thuỷ.<br/>Hoả khắc '''kim'''. '''Kim''' khắc mộc.|Earth bears Metal. Metal enriches Water.<br/>Fire melts Metal. Metal chops Wood.}}

