nó.raw:
===Etymology===
{{rfe|vi}}

Probably originally a variant of {{m|vi|nọ||that yonder}}.<!-- Đó is also a remote demonstrative, hence the same vowel, but they are different series (similar to the attributive {{m|vi|nấy}} vs. nominal {{m|vi|đấy}}), they are related but not "variants" of each other.-->

For semantic relationship, compare {{ncog|fr|il}}, {{ncog|es|él}} (and other reflexes of {{ncog|la|ille}}), {{ncog|mk|тој}}, {{ncog|ja|彼|tr=kare}}, {{ncog|tr|o}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}

===Pronoun===
{{vi-pronoun|[[奴]], [[伮]]}}

# {{lb|vi|impolite|colloquial}} {{l|en|[[he]]; [[him]]; [[she]]; [[her]]}}
#: {{ux|vi|Thằng Tèo đi đâu rồi?<br>Chắc '''nó''' đi chơi với gái rồi.<br>Con Mực đi đâu rồi?<br>Chắc '''nó''' cũng đi kiếm gái luôn.<br>Thế còn con Tũn?<br>'''Nó''' thì tao chịu.|Where's Tèo (a boy)?<br>He's probably going out with girls.<br>Where's Blacky (a dog)?<br>He's probably looking for bitches, too.<br>What about Tũn (a girl)?<br>Dunno about her.}}
# {{lb|vi|literary|fiction|narratology|disrespectful|or|familiar}} {{l|en|[[he]]; [[him]]; [[she]]; [[her]]}} (used by the author when talking about a young person (especially the protagonist) or a non-human animal)
# {{lb|vi|literary}} {{l|en|it}}
#* {{quote-book|vi|year=2012|author=w:Joe Ruelle|title=Ngược chiều vun vút|trans-title=Whooshing toward the Other Way|url=https://books.google.com.vn/books?id=IlpOMwEACAAJ|page=234|text=Ý tôi không phải “phương [[đông|Đông]] – phương [[tây|Tây]]” là cách phân chia [[văn hoá]] vô [[tác dụng]]. Bản thân tôi hay nói “người Tây” thích thế nọ, muốn thế kia – [[đặc biệt]] khi so sánh với [[người Việt]]. Mặc dù không [[chính xác]] lắm nhưng cách đó [[tiết kiệm]] [[thời gian]] cho người viết lẫn người đọc. '''Nó''' súc tích, gòn gọn, đẹp mắt, lôgíc.<br>Nhưng cũng hơi thiếu.|translation=I do not mean that the “Eastern – Western” categorization of cultures is invalid. I often find myself saying “Westerners” like this, want that – especially when comparing with Vietnamese people. Albeit not very accurate, that way [of categorization] doesn’t take much of the writers and the readers’ time [to describe and to understand]. It’s concise, succinct, sightly, logical.<br>But also a little inadequate.}}
# {{lb|vi|colloquial}} [[it]], used to refer to inanimate objects when accompanied by topic-comment structure
#: {{ux|vi|Cái ghế này '''nó''' gãy rồi.|This chair is broken|lit=This chair, '''it''' broke.}} <!-- Vietnamese topic-comment construction is mostly indicated by word order, and a ''thì'' can be inserted after này ("this") but it's not obligatory (and is often left out). "Cái ghế này" is the topic, while nó here is a true pronoun. "Cái ghế này gãy rồi" is a simple sentence without topic-marking. -->

====Usage notes====
* The term is used to refer to any animal (including the human) in the third person, in a casual or disrespectful manner. In usual conversation, the use of pronouns such as {{m|vi|anh ấy}}, {{m|vi|cô ấy}} and the likes when referring to one's peer or younger people would probably sound stiff and artificial (as if from reading a translation). When referring to one's superior or older people, the usage of these pronouns is less marked while the use of {{m|vi|nó}} becomes disrespectful.
* The use of the term to translate the English ''it'', or to refer to an inanimate object, in many cases, is rather artificial, and mostly found in awkward (but common) translations of other languages.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|vi|bọn nó}}; {{l|vi|chúng nó}}; {{l|vi|tụi nó||[[they]]; [[them]]}}

{{cln|vi|personal pronouns|third person pronouns}}

