hết.raw:
===Etymology===
{{inh+|vi|mkh-vie-pro|*heːt}}, from {{inh|vi|aav-pro|*ʔətʔ||[[lack]]; to [[cease]]}}. Cognate with {{cog|thm|-}} ''hɨ́t'' ("all"), {{cog|bdq|ơ̆t||to hold breath, to fast}}, {{cog|mkh-kat-pro|*ʔet||exhausted}} (whence {{cog|kdt|-}} ''ʔɛt'' ("tired")), {{cog|mnw|အိုတ်||to be finished, exhausted}}, {{cog|km|អត់}}, {{cog|pcb|-}} ''ʔet'' ("not"), {{cog|scq|-}} ''ʔeːt'' ("all"), {{cog|ril|-}} [Lang] ''ʔət¹'' ("to stop, to halt").

Insertion of initial ''*h'' in Vietic can also be seen in {{m|mkh-vie-pro|*tŋ-ʔaːm||jaw}} (> {{ncog|vi|hàm}}, Late Vietic), {{m|mkh-vie-pro|*huːɲ||to kiss}} (> {{ncog|vi|hôn}}), {{m|mkh-vie-pro|*hanʔ||he, she, it}} (> {{ncog|vi|hắn}}), {{m|mkh-vie-pro|*haːr||two}} (> {{ncog|vi|hai}}), {{m|mkh-vie-pro|*hoːj||foul-smelling}} (> {{ncog|vi|hôi}}), {{m|mkh-vie-pro|*hɔːŋʔ||river, brook}}, {{m|mkh-vie-pro|*haːŋʔ||to open (mouth)}}.

For the semantic relationship, compare maybe {{ncog|en|over}}, {{m|en|over-}}, {{ncog|de|alle}}, {{m|de|all}}.

