===Etymology===
Fusion between {{m|vi|chăng|t=not any}} and {{m|vi|có|t=to have}}.<ref>{{quote-book|en|author=Trang Phan|title=Studies in Vietnamese Historical Linguistics|chapter=The Rise of Negative Markers in Vietnamese: Evidence from Primary Nôm Texts|passage=It is worth mentioning here that Trinh et al. (2024) put forward a speculation that chẳng is the result of the fusion between chăng and có. The textual data we have examined seems to be in favor of this hypothesis.|year=2024|doi=10.1007/978-981-97-4314-8_3|author2=Tuan-Cuong Nguyen|author3=Masaaki Shimizu}}</ref>

Originally the chief negator in earlier stages of Vietnamese beginning from late 14th century to late seventeenth century; this usage as the neutral negator was replaced by {{m|vi|không}}, but {{m|vi|chẳng}} and other negators are still widely used in many situations. Cognate with {{cog|mtq|chăng}}, {{cog|nuo|chẳng}}. Compare {{cog|th|ช่าง}}, {{cog|lo|ຊ່າງ||to not care}}. See also {{m|vi|chăng}} and {{m|vi|chả}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}
* {{audio|vi|LL-Q9199 (vie)-Penn Zero MSSJ-chẳng.wav|a=Hanoi}}

===Adverb===
{{vi-adv|[[拯]], [[𫽄]], [[庄]], [[庒]]}}

# [[not at all]]
#: {{uxi|vi|'''Chẳng''' biết.|No idea.}}
# [[even if]] [[not]]
#: {{ux|vi|'''Chẳng''' chua, nhưng vẫn có thể là chanh.
|Even if it isn’t sour, it could still be a lemon.}}
# [[if not]]
# [[there is]] [[not]]

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|not at all}} {{l|vi|đâu}}, {{l|vi|nào}}, {{l|vi|không}}, {{l|vi|chả}}, {{l|vi|đếch}} {{qualifier|informal}}, {{l|vi|đách}} {{qualifier|informal}}, {{l|vi|đéo}} {{qualifier|vulgar}}, {{l|vi|cóc}}, {{l|vi|ứ}}

