ngoại giao.raw:
===Alternative forms===
*{{alt|vi|ngoại-giao}}

===Etymology===
{{vi-etym-sino|外|outside|交|to exchange}}, from {{bor|vi|ja|外交|tr=gaikō}}, either clipped from earlier {{m|ja|[[外国]][[交際]]|tr=gaikoku kōsai, {{m|vi|[[ngoại quốc]] [[giao tế]]}}|lit=intercourse with foreign countries}}, a Meiji-era coinage, or merely repurposed from {{der|vi|lzh-lit|外交}}{{attn|lzh|check whether this should be lzh-lit ("Literary Chinese") or lzh ("Classical Chinese", < c. 200 AD)}}. {{cog|lzh-lit|外交}} meant something to the effect of "international relations," rather than the purposeful management of such relations that constitutes "diplomacy" conveyed by {{cog|ja|外交}}.

The "foreign affairs" sense was borrowed from {{bor|vi|zh|外交}}, as in {{m|zh|外交部|tr=Ngoại giao bộ||Ministry of Foreign Affairs}}. The Japanese government uses {{ncog|ja|外務|tr=gaimu}}, as in {{m|ja|外務省|tr=Gaimushō}}, and its Vietnamese cognate, {{m|vi|ngoại vụ}}, is used for provincial {{m|vi||Sở Ngoại vụ|Departments of Foreign Affairs}} subordinate to {{m|vi||Bộ Ngoại giao|the Ministry of Foreign Affairs}}. Compare South {{cog|ko|^외교부}}.

See also {{m|vi|quốc tế}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}

===Noun===
{{vi-noun}}

